1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Now you will hear a speaker talking to a class on a trip to a marine aquarium.

2
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:05,283
Hello, everyone.

3
00:00:05,283 --> 00:00:08,010
Thank you for visiting the aquarium today.

4
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:11,767
I hope you’ve enjoyed seeing the thousands of fish we have here, as well

5
00:00:11,767 --> 00:00:13,020
as the other sea animals.

6
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:16,200
During the next hour we’re going to show you some more sea creatures.

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,110
They all come from a body of water called the Sargasso Sea.

8
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:23,190
The Sargasso Sea is actually part of the Atlantic Ocean.

9
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:26,527
What I mean is---it isn’t a separate body of water;

10
00:00:26,527 --> 00:00:29,030
it’s more like a sea within an ocean.

11
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:32,085
It’s located off the southern half of North America,

12
00:00:32,085 --> 00:00:35,140
and it is very large----it covers millions of kilometers.

13
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:38,180
Now, a couple of things make it distinctive.

14
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:38,230
First,

15
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:44,060
and probably most important, the waters there are very calm—calm and warm.

16
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:46,080
There’s also very little wind there.

17
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:53,030
Surrounding the Sargasso Sea are water currents in the Atlantic that move in a circular motion, counterclockwise.

18
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:55,190
This water swirls around the Sargasso Sea.

19
00:00:55,190 --> 00:00:58,932
Because of the way these currents move, the water in the middle of the

20
00:00:58,932 --> 00:01:01,070
Sargasso Sea doesn’t move much at all.

21
00:01:01,070 --> 00:01:04,958
Because of the still waters and the lack of wind, sailing ships crossing the

22
00:01:04,958 --> 00:01:08,290
Atlantic travel much more slowly when they get to the Sargasso Sea.

23
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:12,270
Without wind, sailboats can get stuck there for long periods.

24
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:18,080
Something else that’s unusual about the Sargasso Sea is the seaweed floating on its surface.

25
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:23,020
Large amounts of a kind of seaweed called sargassum float on top of the water there.

26
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:27,018
And because of the currents, the seaweed stays in the Sargasso Sea, it’s kind

27
00:01:27,018 --> 00:01:29,150
of held in place by the rotating currents.

28
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:32,463
Now this seaweed is what interests us most here at the aquarium,

29
00:01:32,463 --> 00:01:35,499
because it supports all kinds of sea life, like shrimp, crabs,

30
00:01:35,499 --> 00:01:39,916
and fish.Sargassum creates an ecosystem for them to live in.Sargassum creates an ecosystem for

31
00:01:39,916 --> 00:01:41,020
them to live in.

32
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:45,421
What makes this ecosystem quite remarkable is that the creatures there are one you’d

33
00:01:45,421 --> 00:01:49,529
expect to find much closer to shore, much closer to land, not out in

34
00:01:49,529 --> 00:01:51,290
the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.

35
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:56,270
Most likely, sargassum probably did not exist in the Sargasso originally.

36
00:01:56,270 --> 00:02:02,678
Rather, the seaweed and some of the ecosystem it supported drifted out into the Sargasso Sea long ago

37
00:02:02,678 --> 00:02:05,170
and became trapped there by the currents.

38
00:02:05,170 --> 00:02:09,090
From there, it simply spread all over the Sargasso Sea.
